WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:00.720
- Hello, and welcome to Ed Talks,

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:03.600
where we at Khan Academy talk to folks

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:06.560
who are influential in
the field of education.

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:07.410
I'm Kristen DiCerbo,

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:09.580
the chief learning officer
here at Khan Academy.

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:14.040
And I am happy today to welcome Ben Riley,

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.170
who is with Deans for Impact.

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:17.430
And we'll talk a little bit more about

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:19.620
what that organization
is and what it means,

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:23.530
but think about, we want to
talk about how we can embed

00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:24.760
what we know about learning,

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:27.950
what we know from learning
science about how people learn

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:31.340
and how we can help teachers
use that in their classrooms.

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:32.247
So before we get started,

00:01:32.247 --> 00:01:34.480
I want to remind you that Khan Academy

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:36.090
is a non-profit organization,

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:38.780
and we're able to keep doing our work

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:41.840
because of donations from folks like you.

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:43.870
So if you have the means

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:46.240
and you go to khanacademy.org/donate,

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:49.620
you will find a place where
you can help us continue

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:52.430
with our mission, thank you very much.

00:01:52.430 --> 00:01:54.400
And I also want to recognize

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:57.380
some of our COVID crisis supporters,

00:01:57.380 --> 00:02:00.470
including AT&amp;T, General Motors and Fastly.

00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:01.820
Thank you for your support.

00:02:03.050 --> 00:02:04.900
One final thing before we get started,

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:08.170
if you want to revisit
some of these conversations

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:10.960
in audio form, we do have a podcast,

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:12.620
Homeroom with Sal, The Podcast

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:15.660
that you can find wherever
you get your podcasts,

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:20.110
and you can enjoy listening
to some of our conversations.

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:24.140
And we will jump right in
now to talking to Ben Riley,

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:28.270
Ben, welcome and thank
you for joining us today.

00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:29.870
- Thanks for having me, Kristen.

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:31.563
- Excellent.

00:02:31.563 --> 00:02:34.710
So I like to start off
with just talking to people

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:37.960
about how they got to
where they are today.

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:40.130
So I know in your background,

00:02:40.130 --> 00:02:43.560
you've got some different
paths that maybe aren't typical

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:45.690
for folks that are in education.

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:49.601
Tell us a little bit
how you got to wind up

00:02:49.601 --> 00:02:51.223
in the field of education.

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:53.760
- Sure.

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:56.770
So I come from a family
of public educators.

00:02:56.770 --> 00:02:59.760
My grandmother was a school psychologist.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:04.597
My mother was a school teacher
and then a school librarian,

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:07.670
and then a school counselor.

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:11.040
My father was a neuroscientist
and a professor.

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:13.720
So education was everywhere in my family.

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:17.180
And as a consequence, I
wanted nothing to do with it.

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:19.120
My whole plan with my life was

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:22.240
to do something entirely
different from that.

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:24.070
And through a circuitous route,

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:25.410
I ended up getting a law degree.

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:27.560
And even though I didn't
want to be a lawyer either,

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:31.530
I ended up working for the
California Department of Justice.

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:36.450
And within that, I started
working on lawsuits

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:39.020
brought against the State of California

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:42.090
for issues related to education policy.

00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:44.190
And maybe it was in the blood.

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:46.320
Maybe it was something
about being surrounded

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:47.700
by educators my whole life,

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:51.510
but the questions that were
raised in those lawsuits

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:53.700
were profoundly interesting to me.

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:57.320
And so I quickly became
pretty heavily involved

00:03:57.320 --> 00:04:01.530
in the issues related to
California education policy

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:03.760
that were hotly contested at the time.

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:04.990
Charter schools,

00:04:04.990 --> 00:04:09.580
should every eighth grader
be taught algebra or not,

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:13.160
should testing be only in
English or in multiple languages,

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:15.230
a lot of hot button issues.

00:04:15.230 --> 00:04:18.440
And then there were policy
matters that started happening

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:22.140
like race to the top and
president Obama wanting

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:25.470
to change education policy
throughout the 50 states.

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:29.177
And soon to be governor
again, Jerry Brown asking me,

00:04:29.177 --> 00:04:32.640
"Is it a good idea, is it a
bad idea, what's your view?"

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:36.470
And so that kind of sucked
me into education issues

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:40.490
and I felt a bit hamstrung
being an attorney

00:04:40.490 --> 00:04:42.820
where I was forced to
sort of take the position

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:44.040
of the State of California,

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:47.910
which wasn't always what I
necessarily personally believed

00:04:47.910 --> 00:04:51.570
and aligned with and through
a bunch of circuitous routes

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:53.420
that I won't belabor,

00:04:53.420 --> 00:04:56.400
I ended up becoming really interested

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:00.780
in how to improve teaching
and teachers in particular.

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:03.020
And so what I always
say when I speak is that

00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:05.250
I don't have a background as a teacher.

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:07.090
I wouldn't pretend to be a teacher,

00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:08.930
but I have spent my life as an advocate

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:11.430
and I am an advocate for
teachers and for great teaching.

00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:14.130
And so that's what I've found myself doing

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:15.833
for most of my professional life.

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:19.750
- That's great, so my mom
was also a school librarian.

00:05:19.750 --> 00:05:21.450
I've talked to other people in education

00:05:21.450 --> 00:05:23.110
whose mothers are school librarians.

00:05:23.110 --> 00:05:27.620
I think that's a powerful force
to have in your background

00:05:27.620 --> 00:05:29.933
that kind of just gets you into these

00:05:29.933 --> 00:05:31.264
and hooks you into these issues

00:05:31.264 --> 00:05:34.823
and ideas that you wouldn't
expect to come back to.

00:05:37.217 --> 00:05:41.450
So that's excellent, so based
on that, let's just talk,

00:05:41.450 --> 00:05:44.900
tell me a little bit about
what Deans for Impact is

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:48.463
as an organization and what
prompted you to found it.

00:05:49.310 --> 00:05:52.320
- Yeah, so Deans for Impact is

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:55.610
a national nonprofit organization
dedicated to ensuring

00:05:55.610 --> 00:05:58.110
that every child has a
well-prepared teacher

00:05:58.110 --> 00:06:01.730
and the story of our organizational life

00:06:01.730 --> 00:06:04.040
is a little different, I think, than some

00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:07.580
in that it didn't start out
with, at least from my end,

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:10.430
I had no intent of
starting an organization.

00:06:10.430 --> 00:06:12.050
What happened was that

00:06:12.050 --> 00:06:14.340
because of those interests
I mentioned earlier,

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:15.670
I started to get interested in like,

00:06:15.670 --> 00:06:19.310
who are the leaders in
programs that prepare people

00:06:19.310 --> 00:06:21.390
to be teachers, the deans of schools

00:06:21.390 --> 00:06:23.610
of education, predominantly.

00:06:23.610 --> 00:06:24.860
Who might be interested in trying

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:27.780
to transform and improve that process.

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:31.510
And I started to look
for them and found them,

00:06:31.510 --> 00:06:33.710
and they started leading
me and mentioning others.

00:06:33.710 --> 00:06:36.740
But what was fascinating is,
as I was talking to them,

00:06:36.740 --> 00:06:37.970
it was very, very clear to me

00:06:37.970 --> 00:06:41.490
that there was not any
connecting network amongst them.

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:43.750
And so I tried to bring them together

00:06:43.750 --> 00:06:45.540
and just put them in a room.

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:48.510
This was going on 8, 9, 10 years ago

00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:50.050
and say, "Okay, well, you know,

00:06:50.050 --> 00:06:52.140
everybody in this room seems to agree

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:55.040
that we can do better in the
way that we prepare teachers.

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:56.410
What will it take to do that?

00:06:56.410 --> 00:06:58.480
And what would an organization dedicated

00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:00.510
to making that happen look like?"

00:07:00.510 --> 00:07:02.950
And from that, Deans for Impact was born

00:07:02.950 --> 00:07:06.800
and a big part of that, we
do a lot of different things,

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:09.320
but a big part of that has been trying

00:07:09.320 --> 00:07:11.820
to strengthen the experiences that people

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:14.660
who are planning on becoming teachers,

00:07:14.660 --> 00:07:15.940
the experiences that they have,

00:07:15.940 --> 00:07:18.290
making them more meaningful and relevant

00:07:18.290 --> 00:07:21.080
to classroom practice
and connecting to them

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:25.150
to our best scientific
understanding of how students learn.

00:07:25.150 --> 00:07:26.980
And I would say that if there's anything

00:07:26.980 --> 00:07:30.070
that I feel like was my contribution

00:07:30.070 --> 00:07:31.690
to the Deans for Impact effort,

00:07:31.690 --> 00:07:35.120
it was really focusing on
that piece in particular,

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:39.410
in part because I was stunned
to find out some years

00:07:39.410 --> 00:07:40.670
into my career in education,

00:07:40.670 --> 00:07:44.110
that there were all these
insights into how our minds work

00:07:44.110 --> 00:07:46.880
and how we think and learn,
that I was not aware of.

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:49.700
And frankly, was working
on pursuing some projects

00:07:49.700 --> 00:07:52.410
that looking back on it,
I might not have worked on

00:07:52.410 --> 00:07:55.060
and pursued if I had known
the science of learning.

00:07:55.060 --> 00:07:56.480
So we've made that a point of emphasis

00:07:56.480 --> 00:08:00.200
and it continues to be something
we're working very hard at.

00:08:00.200 --> 00:08:02.240
- That's actually how I
first came to be aware

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:05.390
of Deans for Impact was
the release of this science

00:08:05.390 --> 00:08:09.650
of learning paper that laid
out in really clear language,

00:08:09.650 --> 00:08:11.620
some fundamental things

00:08:11.620 --> 00:08:15.190
that we know about learning from research.

00:08:15.190 --> 00:08:17.450
Nice little footnotes
that give the reference

00:08:17.450 --> 00:08:18.540
to where things are,

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:21.080
but that it's really accessible and easy

00:08:21.080 --> 00:08:24.970
for people who haven't studied them

00:08:24.970 --> 00:08:27.050
and don't have PhDs in education

00:08:27.050 --> 00:08:30.090
to really grasp onto,
how did that come about

00:08:31.410 --> 00:08:33.903
as you started thinking
about how to build that?

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:37.430
- Yeah, thanks for those kind words.

00:08:37.430 --> 00:08:39.040
There were a couple of things

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:41.430
where I could almost say we got lucky,

00:08:41.430 --> 00:08:43.390
but it was also a little bit by design

00:08:43.390 --> 00:08:45.980
in putting the science of learning out.

00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:47.280
And by the way, it's available

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:50.050
at the Deans for Impact website,
completely free of charge,

00:08:50.050 --> 00:08:52.483
six pages, click download, you've got it.

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:55.400
And really it was born of the fact

00:08:55.400 --> 00:08:57.940
that I knew we had a cognitive scientist

00:08:57.940 --> 00:09:01.330
who thought a lot about
how to communicate ideas

00:09:01.330 --> 00:09:04.060
of cognitive science to the general public

00:09:04.060 --> 00:09:07.260
and Dan Willingham at the
University of Virginia is someone

00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:10.060
who's written a lot of
books, a lot of blog posts.

00:09:10.060 --> 00:09:11.890
And the reason I knew about--

00:09:11.890 --> 00:09:13.900
- He's actually a previous Ed Talk guest.

00:09:13.900 --> 00:09:15.833
- Okay, great, I will watch them

00:09:15.833 --> 00:09:17.373
because I'm sure it was compelling.

00:09:17.373 --> 00:09:19.150
I didn't even know that.

00:09:19.150 --> 00:09:21.280
And that I think speaks to the fact

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:25.010
that Dan has spent a
non-trivial amount of his career

00:09:25.010 --> 00:09:27.210
trying to get these ideas
in the hands of teachers.

00:09:27.210 --> 00:09:28.950
So I knew on the science side,

00:09:28.950 --> 00:09:31.240
we had a scientist who could think

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:34.300
how to communicate
effectively to non-scientists.

00:09:34.300 --> 00:09:36.290
The flip side and this is I think a place

00:09:36.290 --> 00:09:38.900
that's still underdeveloped
in education generally

00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:41.860
is that there's an awful
lot of teachers out there

00:09:41.860 --> 00:09:44.800
and wouldn't it be interesting
to have a teacher involved

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:47.010
in the creating of something
that's meant for teachers?

00:09:47.010 --> 00:09:49.430
And so, through, again,

00:09:49.430 --> 00:09:52.650
a time when blogging was
more common than it is now,

00:09:52.650 --> 00:09:55.410
I had found this blogging
teacher named Paul Bruno,

00:09:55.410 --> 00:09:57.960
based in California, middle
school science teacher,

00:09:59.060 --> 00:10:01.650
who wrote often about education issues

00:10:01.650 --> 00:10:04.710
and wrote very clearly and
had written in particular

00:10:04.710 --> 00:10:07.850
about academic standards,
content standards,

00:10:07.850 --> 00:10:11.480
and how some of the new
standards that were being drafted

00:10:11.480 --> 00:10:13.250
weren't gonna be very useful to teachers

00:10:13.250 --> 00:10:15.070
because they were getting
vaguer and vaguer.

00:10:15.070 --> 00:10:16.860
And I thought, this is what we need.

00:10:16.860 --> 00:10:20.140
We need to find something that
will tell teachers in ways

00:10:20.140 --> 00:10:23.430
that don't make it just
completely like anything goes,

00:10:23.430 --> 00:10:26.470
which is often, I think
the unfortunate by-product

00:10:26.470 --> 00:10:28.620
of people being afraid to take a stand.

00:10:28.620 --> 00:10:30.610
And yet at the same time, you don't want

00:10:30.610 --> 00:10:32.493
to get so micro specific that it sounds

00:10:32.493 --> 00:10:35.390
like you're being prescriptive
in how you are teaching.

00:10:35.390 --> 00:10:38.167
And so we got Paul and
Dan together and said,

00:10:38.167 --> 00:10:39.810
"All right, let's get to work."

00:10:39.810 --> 00:10:42.210
And from that, the science
of learning was born.

00:10:44.310 --> 00:10:45.360
- That's fantastic.

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:46.960
That point is really interesting

00:10:46.960 --> 00:10:49.190
because often even researchers themselves

00:10:49.190 --> 00:10:52.640
very much hedge their conclusions
or say, "Well, you know,

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:56.280
it might depend or in
this specific situation."

00:10:56.280 --> 00:11:01.130
And getting from that to something
that is still actionable,

00:11:01.130 --> 00:11:03.890
knowing that there's maybe
some caveats behind the scene,

00:11:03.890 --> 00:11:06.170
but it gives teachers something

00:11:06.170 --> 00:11:09.670
that is concrete for them to work on,

00:11:09.670 --> 00:11:14.030
but doesn't get all the way
to "Do this exact thing",

00:11:14.030 --> 00:11:17.160
that's a fine line to
walk and where things fit.

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:19.870
- It's hard to find that
sweet spot for sure.

00:11:19.870 --> 00:11:23.240
And I think that education
generally, again,

00:11:23.240 --> 00:11:26.810
sort of tends towards the nice,

00:11:26.810 --> 00:11:28.210
and that's a good thing in general,

00:11:28.210 --> 00:11:30.540
we're working with students,
we want to be nice,

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:35.180
but I think teachers
sometimes need something

00:11:35.180 --> 00:11:38.150
that's more directly relevant
so that they don't have

00:11:38.150 --> 00:11:40.690
to be spending, they've
got so much time to spend

00:11:40.690 --> 00:11:43.840
on trying to think through all
the various things they need

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:45.370
to think through.

00:11:45.370 --> 00:11:47.277
If you could make it
easier for them to see,

00:11:47.277 --> 00:11:51.150
"Okay, this is how this
scientific principle connects

00:11:51.150 --> 00:11:55.200
to specific teaching actions,
instructional decisions."

00:11:55.200 --> 00:11:58.010
Then you sort of help
build the bridge, scaffold,

00:11:58.010 --> 00:12:00.190
as we say, in education,

00:12:00.190 --> 00:12:03.010
ideas from science to
their implementation,

00:12:03.010 --> 00:12:05.890
their application in the classroom.

00:12:05.890 --> 00:12:08.190
So I think that's an ongoing

00:12:08.190 --> 00:12:11.410
and will never be a perfected challenge.

00:12:11.410 --> 00:12:15.720
But I think that if you have,
again, teachers involved,

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:17.420
you're gonna be more
likely to get something

00:12:17.420 --> 00:12:19.010
that teachers find helpful.

00:12:19.010 --> 00:12:21.040
And also, hopefully you have scientists

00:12:21.040 --> 00:12:23.610
who are able to think about how to make

00:12:23.610 --> 00:12:28.373
that science translatable and
understandable to non-experts.

00:12:29.230 --> 00:12:31.010
- That makes sense, that makes sense.

00:12:31.010 --> 00:12:33.750
So in the research world,

00:12:33.750 --> 00:12:35.470
sometimes we're starting to hear a lot

00:12:35.470 --> 00:12:39.560
about brain science and brains,

00:12:39.560 --> 00:12:42.340
but you've been vocal about
distinguishing between the brain

00:12:42.340 --> 00:12:45.030
and the mind when it comes
to children's learning.

00:12:45.030 --> 00:12:47.570
What's the difference between those two?

00:12:47.570 --> 00:12:49.923
- Yeah, thank you for that question,

00:12:49.923 --> 00:12:52.037
because I see this a lot
too, as you just said,

00:12:52.037 --> 00:12:53.870
and there's conferences that are like

00:12:53.870 --> 00:12:56.992
mind, comma, brain, comma blank.

00:12:56.992 --> 00:13:00.220
And a lot of times people
will assume that like,

00:13:00.220 --> 00:13:02.990
I'm really excited about neuroscience

00:13:02.990 --> 00:13:04.810
and my dad was a neuroscientist.

00:13:04.810 --> 00:13:07.270
I actually think neuroscience
is super fascinating

00:13:07.270 --> 00:13:10.070
as a field and provocatively,

00:13:10.070 --> 00:13:12.020
I actually don't think it's
that useful to teachers.

00:13:12.020 --> 00:13:14.883
So let me back up and
answer your question.

00:13:15.840 --> 00:13:18.630
The brain is an organ, right?

00:13:18.630 --> 00:13:20.830
It exists inside our head

00:13:20.830 --> 00:13:22.620
and there's no doubt that it exists.

00:13:22.620 --> 00:13:25.730
It has all the different
pieces, the cerebellum,

00:13:25.730 --> 00:13:28.930
the amygdala, and too
many other parts to count,

00:13:28.930 --> 00:13:30.750
but it's there, it's a thing.

00:13:30.750 --> 00:13:33.930
It's a physical object
that's part of our anatomy.

00:13:33.930 --> 00:13:36.130
The mind is a metaphor.

00:13:36.130 --> 00:13:41.130
The mind is a construct that
we use to try to make sense

00:13:41.970 --> 00:13:44.900
of a lot of different
things that are going on.

00:13:44.900 --> 00:13:47.120
Sometimes when I try to explain this,

00:13:47.120 --> 00:13:49.120
I invoke another metaphor

00:13:49.120 --> 00:13:53.060
or analogy to make this a
little bit more tangible.

00:13:53.060 --> 00:13:54.820
So I'll try that out here.

00:13:54.820 --> 00:13:56.970
If you think about a car

00:13:56.970 --> 00:13:58.800
and what a car is supposed to do,

00:13:58.800 --> 00:14:03.530
a car is supposed to transport
you from one spot to another,

00:14:03.530 --> 00:14:06.810
the car has within it an engine.

00:14:06.810 --> 00:14:09.290
We're gonna call that engine the brain.

00:14:09.290 --> 00:14:12.870
And what I'm gonna say is
in order to operate your car

00:14:12.870 --> 00:14:15.660
in ways that are meaningful
and useful to you,

00:14:15.660 --> 00:14:17.700
you don't really need
to know hardly anything

00:14:17.700 --> 00:14:20.420
about how that engine works.

00:14:20.420 --> 00:14:24.400
What you do need to know
is how to drive the car,

00:14:24.400 --> 00:14:26.190
driving is what matters.

00:14:26.190 --> 00:14:28.970
And if you think about what
we mean when we say driving,

00:14:28.970 --> 00:14:31.390
it actually consists of a
lot of different pieces.

00:14:31.390 --> 00:14:33.370
It's putting your foot down on the gas

00:14:33.370 --> 00:14:36.970
and shifting into gear
and turning and braking

00:14:36.970 --> 00:14:40.130
and making decisions
about oncoming traffic.

00:14:40.130 --> 00:14:41.710
Driving is the mind,

00:14:41.710 --> 00:14:46.490
the mind is the driver and
the driving of cognition

00:14:46.490 --> 00:14:48.320
and how we make sense of the world.

00:14:48.320 --> 00:14:52.130
And so everything that I
think that is super important

00:14:52.130 --> 00:14:55.380
for teachers to have at
least some understanding of

00:14:55.380 --> 00:14:57.900
is how do I drive this thing?

00:14:57.900 --> 00:15:01.390
And the thing really is the
minds of their students.

00:15:01.390 --> 00:15:03.190
How do I get them to think about

00:15:03.190 --> 00:15:05.310
what it is they need to think about

00:15:05.310 --> 00:15:08.113
in ways that lead to durable
and lasting learning?

00:15:10.350 --> 00:15:11.720
- That totally makes sense.

00:15:11.720 --> 00:15:15.061
And I could probably go
on a whole other tangent

00:15:15.061 --> 00:15:17.680
about the brain science piece.

00:15:17.680 --> 00:15:19.740
I actually am very aligned with you,

00:15:19.740 --> 00:15:22.466
the practicalities of
what it offers for folks

00:15:22.466 --> 00:15:25.160
in classrooms and what they're doing.

00:15:25.160 --> 00:15:29.220
But as you're thinking about what teachers

00:15:29.220 --> 00:15:33.620
should know about how to drive
that car and how to do it,

00:15:33.620 --> 00:15:35.870
I know that you also did some work

00:15:35.870 --> 00:15:37.210
with some pre-service teachers,

00:15:37.210 --> 00:15:39.120
teachers that are in
teacher training programs

00:15:39.120 --> 00:15:42.490
to try to understand
what are they learning

00:15:42.490 --> 00:15:45.570
and what do they know as they
go through those programs.

00:15:45.570 --> 00:15:47.470
What were some of your findings there?

00:15:48.330 --> 00:15:51.300
- Yeah, so before directly
answering that question,

00:15:51.300 --> 00:15:52.860
I'll back up and say that

00:15:52.860 --> 00:15:55.000
we referenced the science
of learning document

00:15:55.000 --> 00:15:57.590
and that document sort of
has a series of questions

00:15:57.590 --> 00:15:59.640
about how learning takes place.

00:15:59.640 --> 00:16:01.900
How do we understand new ideas?

00:16:01.900 --> 00:16:04.730
We, over time, sort of distilled those

00:16:04.730 --> 00:16:07.040
into a set of principles

00:16:07.040 --> 00:16:09.170
that come from the science of learning,

00:16:09.170 --> 00:16:11.930
the science of cognition,
cognitive science.

00:16:11.930 --> 00:16:15.530
And we used those as sort
of our basic framework

00:16:15.530 --> 00:16:19.830
for figuring out what
it is that we both want

00:16:19.830 --> 00:16:23.190
future teachers to know
and where do they start?

00:16:23.190 --> 00:16:25.560
What do they know about how we learn

00:16:25.560 --> 00:16:28.320
before anything has happened
vis-a-vis an engagement

00:16:28.320 --> 00:16:30.520
with Deans for Impact, or frankly,

00:16:30.520 --> 00:16:32.330
in any of the programs
that are preparing them

00:16:32.330 --> 00:16:33.800
to become teachers.

00:16:33.800 --> 00:16:37.370
And so we developed an assessment and we

00:16:37.370 --> 00:16:39.340
actually looked around
to see if any assessment

00:16:39.340 --> 00:16:42.170
like this existed already,
and it might be out there,

00:16:42.170 --> 00:16:43.160
but we couldn't find it.

00:16:43.160 --> 00:16:48.160
So one of our very able employees,

00:16:48.940 --> 00:16:51.687
Dr. Rebecca Berlin came on board and said,

00:16:51.687 --> 00:16:53.450
"No problem, let's get to work.

00:16:53.450 --> 00:16:54.380
We'll build an assessment

00:16:54.380 --> 00:16:56.240
around these learning science principles."

00:16:56.240 --> 00:16:57.400
And so we did,

00:16:57.400 --> 00:17:01.740
and then we asked a lot
of teacher candidates,

00:17:01.740 --> 00:17:03.390
I think, a little over a thousand

00:17:04.272 --> 00:17:06.930
to take the assessment
and give us just a sense

00:17:06.930 --> 00:17:09.640
of where they were in their understanding

00:17:09.640 --> 00:17:10.473
of learning science.

00:17:10.473 --> 00:17:12.480
And there was a couple
of interesting findings.

00:17:12.480 --> 00:17:16.660
You can go kind of glass half
empty, glass half full in that

00:17:16.660 --> 00:17:19.730
when it came to sort of basic
understanding of cognition,

00:17:19.730 --> 00:17:21.480
they got about half the questions right,

00:17:21.480 --> 00:17:22.740
half of them wrong.

00:17:22.740 --> 00:17:24.070
Now, you could say,

00:17:24.070 --> 00:17:27.557
half right on a test is
usually no an A for sure.

00:17:27.557 --> 00:17:29.210
And is usually tilted
towards the other end

00:17:29.210 --> 00:17:30.890
of that spectrum, on the other hand,

00:17:30.890 --> 00:17:33.900
they hadn't really been
exposed to a lot of it yet.

00:17:33.900 --> 00:17:37.240
So, how much would you
expect them to know anyway,

00:17:37.240 --> 00:17:40.350
if this hasn't been a
focus of their studies?

00:17:40.350 --> 00:17:42.040
The other thing that I
thought was really interesting

00:17:42.040 --> 00:17:45.460
with that is that there
was significant difference

00:17:45.460 --> 00:17:48.780
between their understanding
of scientific principles

00:17:48.780 --> 00:17:51.720
in the abstract versus
their understanding of how

00:17:51.720 --> 00:17:53.500
to apply those principles when making

00:17:53.500 --> 00:17:55.160
an instructional decision,

00:17:55.160 --> 00:17:56.790
something that relates to the classroom.

00:17:56.790 --> 00:18:00.060
And on those sorts of test
questions that we gave them,

00:18:00.060 --> 00:18:02.470
they did a little bit
better, significantly better,

00:18:02.470 --> 00:18:05.460
I would say, 10, 15
percentage points better.

00:18:05.460 --> 00:18:07.970
So that was interesting
because it sort of suggests,

00:18:07.970 --> 00:18:09.850
and I've come to believe this in my time

00:18:09.850 --> 00:18:12.770
working in education, that

00:18:12.770 --> 00:18:16.220
sometimes you don't necessarily
know why something works.

00:18:16.220 --> 00:18:17.940
You just figure out that it works.

00:18:17.940 --> 00:18:21.640
And I think when looking at
sort of making certain choices,

00:18:21.640 --> 00:18:25.000
people who are preparing
to become teachers

00:18:25.000 --> 00:18:27.207
are better able to figure out

00:18:27.207 --> 00:18:29.400
"Well, yeah, I want to
do that with my students

00:18:29.400 --> 00:18:32.440
rather than some alternative."

00:18:32.440 --> 00:18:34.660
And they make a better choice there

00:18:34.660 --> 00:18:36.630
than their basic understanding

00:18:36.630 --> 00:18:38.320
of learning science might suggest.

00:18:38.320 --> 00:18:39.600
So that was encouraging.

00:18:39.600 --> 00:18:42.880
And in some ways we're trying to lift up

00:18:42.880 --> 00:18:46.400
how future teachers do on both dimensions,

00:18:46.400 --> 00:18:48.610
but it's really that latter dimension

00:18:48.610 --> 00:18:51.400
that is applying these
principles in practice

00:18:51.400 --> 00:18:53.820
where the real impact will be made.

00:18:53.820 --> 00:18:57.540
Because if they are making those
sorts of choices correctly,

00:18:57.540 --> 00:18:59.660
over and over again with their students,

00:18:59.660 --> 00:19:02.140
it's more likely to
have this sort of impact

00:19:02.140 --> 00:19:04.730
on student learning that
we're hoping to have.

00:19:04.730 --> 00:19:07.070
So, some people can look at the data

00:19:07.070 --> 00:19:08.970
and sort of have a negative takeaway,

00:19:08.970 --> 00:19:12.090
but we view it the opposite
at Deans for Impact.

00:19:12.090 --> 00:19:12.923
- Yeah, well,

00:19:12.923 --> 00:19:15.180
it also somewhat laid out the case

00:19:15.180 --> 00:19:17.493
for why your organization is needed.

00:19:19.610 --> 00:19:21.507
People are doing okay on their own

00:19:21.507 --> 00:19:25.830
and we need to think about
how to raise that up above

00:19:25.830 --> 00:19:27.410
where it is, that makes sense.

00:19:27.410 --> 00:19:32.270
I also wonder if some of the
results from education research

00:19:32.270 --> 00:19:34.850
are kind of intuitive
and what we would expect.

00:19:34.850 --> 00:19:37.230
And so if you're making
choices based on that,

00:19:37.230 --> 00:19:38.299
it makes sense.

00:19:38.299 --> 00:19:39.132
And then there's some other things

00:19:39.132 --> 00:19:41.663
that maybe aren't as intuitive,

00:19:41.663 --> 00:19:44.060
honestly, learning styles comes to mind

00:19:44.060 --> 00:19:46.230
where you think like, "Oh yeah,

00:19:46.230 --> 00:19:48.290
people have these different styles

00:19:48.290 --> 00:19:49.680
and we should tailor instruction to that."

00:19:49.680 --> 00:19:52.220
But the research doesn't
really support it.

00:19:52.220 --> 00:19:53.670
And so maybe it's more of those kinds

00:19:53.670 --> 00:19:57.490
of counter-intuitive
findings where you're seeing

00:19:57.490 --> 00:19:59.080
if no one's ever been taught that,

00:19:59.080 --> 00:20:02.883
then they aren't gonna know
where that intuition is.

00:20:04.094 --> 00:20:05.823
Just kind of thinking about the results.

00:20:05.823 --> 00:20:06.780
- I think you're right,

00:20:06.780 --> 00:20:09.100
now, I'm gonna make it a
little bit provocative here,

00:20:09.100 --> 00:20:11.320
'cause I'm gonna ask you a question.

00:20:11.320 --> 00:20:13.760
So before we jumped on this,

00:20:13.760 --> 00:20:16.160
I went and visited the
Khan Academy website,

00:20:16.160 --> 00:20:18.140
which I hadn't been to in a while.

00:20:18.140 --> 00:20:21.930
And like many, many places, I see sort of

00:20:23.219 --> 00:20:24.940
a cry for personalized learning.

00:20:24.940 --> 00:20:26.940
A sort of flag for personalized learning.

00:20:26.940 --> 00:20:27.773
And in particular,

00:20:27.773 --> 00:20:31.830
that students should control
the pace of their learning.

00:20:31.830 --> 00:20:33.770
And look, you're not responsible

00:20:33.770 --> 00:20:35.320
for what's put on a website, I know that,

00:20:35.320 --> 00:20:38.748
but I wonder knowing what you
know about learning science,

00:20:38.748 --> 00:20:40.780
do you think that's a good idea?

00:20:40.780 --> 00:20:42.430
Because I think that's another thing where

00:20:42.430 --> 00:20:44.750
that idea sounds so appealing.

00:20:44.750 --> 00:20:47.279
Certainly philanthropists enjoy it.

00:20:47.279 --> 00:20:48.920
I think a lot of parents enjoy it.

00:20:48.920 --> 00:20:52.570
And provocatively, I
would say the evidence

00:20:52.570 --> 00:20:55.120
as I understand it from
learning science would suggest

00:20:55.120 --> 00:21:00.120
that actually novices,
students are in a poor position

00:21:00.640 --> 00:21:02.270
to be regulators of their own learning

00:21:02.270 --> 00:21:04.640
because they don't know
what they don't know.

00:21:04.640 --> 00:21:06.670
And thinking is very difficult.

00:21:06.670 --> 00:21:08.470
And they, when faced with something

00:21:08.470 --> 00:21:10.270
that is cognitively challenging,

00:21:10.270 --> 00:21:13.160
they may try to take the
path of least resistance

00:21:13.160 --> 00:21:15.360
in order to get through that.

00:21:15.360 --> 00:21:17.770
I mean, I certainly see this
a lot with students who just,

00:21:17.770 --> 00:21:20.190
they just want to get the
answer right and move on.

00:21:20.190 --> 00:21:24.460
And that's kind of built into
a lot of our education system.

00:21:24.460 --> 00:21:27.480
So I wanted to ask you if
you are working internally,

00:21:27.480 --> 00:21:29.790
if you have like internal conversations,

00:21:29.790 --> 00:21:32.970
how do you think that situates
between learning science

00:21:32.970 --> 00:21:34.513
and personalized learning?

00:21:35.390 --> 00:21:36.370
- First, love it.

00:21:36.370 --> 00:21:37.503
Thank you for asking.

00:21:38.460 --> 00:21:41.420
So we have tons of
conversations about this.

00:21:41.420 --> 00:21:44.520
So the foundational
thing that Khan Academy

00:21:44.520 --> 00:21:46.960
is really based on is the
idea of mastery learning.

00:21:46.960 --> 00:21:49.390
And so the idea of mastery learning,

00:21:49.390 --> 00:21:53.209
working at something until you

00:21:53.209 --> 00:21:57.280
have that high level of
proficiency before you go on

00:21:57.280 --> 00:22:00.010
to the next thing that you're working on,

00:22:00.010 --> 00:22:01.880
that is pretty well supported.

00:22:01.880 --> 00:22:05.180
There's meta-analyses and
meta-analyses, and meta-analyses

00:22:05.180 --> 00:22:08.240
that support that idea
of mastery learning.

00:22:08.240 --> 00:22:12.640
And the key thing about
mastery learning is that

00:22:12.640 --> 00:22:15.670
it's pretty antithetical to
moving at a lockstep pace

00:22:15.670 --> 00:22:18.320
in a classroom because it's just,

00:22:18.320 --> 00:22:20.060
students are gonna have different gaps

00:22:20.060 --> 00:22:21.140
that they need to fill in.

00:22:21.140 --> 00:22:24.270
Some students are gonna
take longer on this concept

00:22:24.270 --> 00:22:25.350
to really master it.

00:22:25.350 --> 00:22:27.860
Then the students who are gonna take

00:22:27.860 --> 00:22:29.460
longer at this concept.

00:22:29.460 --> 00:22:32.770
So that idea of pacing
and students maybe being

00:22:32.770 --> 00:22:35.980
at different paces is
really an artifact for us

00:22:35.980 --> 00:22:39.370
of the idea that we want
students to get to that idea

00:22:39.370 --> 00:22:42.670
of mastery and really build
proficiency on a skill

00:22:42.670 --> 00:22:46.080
before they try to build
something on top of that skill,

00:22:46.080 --> 00:22:48.660
with that shaky foundation
of where things are.

00:22:48.660 --> 00:22:50.600
So there's that piece of it.

00:22:50.600 --> 00:22:52.060
And then the second question is,

00:22:52.060 --> 00:22:55.960
okay, that's kind of that
personalized piece of,

00:22:55.960 --> 00:22:58.900
everyone's not gonna be
moving at the same pace

00:22:58.900 --> 00:23:00.110
through this content,

00:23:00.110 --> 00:23:01.830
you're gonna move at
different paces depending

00:23:01.830 --> 00:23:02.663
on what you're doing.

00:23:02.663 --> 00:23:05.110
And then the question is
who controls that pace

00:23:05.110 --> 00:23:07.000
and who controls what's doing?

00:23:07.000 --> 00:23:11.210
So I think, what I pushed for is actually,

00:23:11.210 --> 00:23:14.060
there's a kind of a triangle of teacher,

00:23:14.060 --> 00:23:16.360
the system and the learner.

00:23:16.360 --> 00:23:20.110
And both, depending on
a student's proficiency,

00:23:20.110 --> 00:23:22.670
depending on their developmental stage,

00:23:22.670 --> 00:23:26.330
depending on where they
are, we want to be changing

00:23:27.640 --> 00:23:31.820
that portion of who's
doing what over time.

00:23:31.820 --> 00:23:33.650
And when we're starting out with novices,

00:23:33.650 --> 00:23:35.800
when we're starting out
with young learners,

00:23:35.800 --> 00:23:37.890
it's gonna be more teacher directed.

00:23:37.890 --> 00:23:41.090
And when we have teachers who
have 30 kids in a classroom,

00:23:41.090 --> 00:23:43.780
there's some benefit to having
the system do a little bit

00:23:43.780 --> 00:23:47.460
of guidance too, that
we can build into that.

00:23:47.460 --> 00:23:50.615
Over time, ideally we want learners

00:23:50.615 --> 00:23:54.440
to end up being lifelong
learners who are able

00:23:54.440 --> 00:23:56.660
to control and regulate
their own learning,

00:23:56.660 --> 00:23:58.530
but they're not gonna be
there from the beginning.

00:23:58.530 --> 00:24:01.630
So it's more of a journey
and that self-regulated

00:24:01.630 --> 00:24:04.920
personalized learner is the goal,

00:24:04.920 --> 00:24:06.990
but not where we're
gonna be all at the time.

00:24:06.990 --> 00:24:09.637
So that's kind of where we
are, does that make sense?

00:24:09.637 --> 00:24:10.470
- No, it makes sense.

00:24:10.470 --> 00:24:11.303
And I think, look,

00:24:11.303 --> 00:24:14.680
I can be that rude guest
who's sort of invited on

00:24:14.680 --> 00:24:17.200
and then like lobs little criticisms,

00:24:17.200 --> 00:24:20.010
but I understand where
you're coming from with that.

00:24:20.010 --> 00:24:23.300
I would say that like, the mastery point

00:24:23.300 --> 00:24:25.460
is an interesting one because we could,

00:24:25.460 --> 00:24:28.460
offline or in a perhaps
future Ed Talk talk about

00:24:28.460 --> 00:24:29.810
various readings of the research.

00:24:29.810 --> 00:24:32.250
I think it's really hard to know

00:24:32.250 --> 00:24:34.520
what mastery is of anything.

00:24:34.520 --> 00:24:37.940
And I think one of the key
insights of learning science

00:24:37.940 --> 00:24:39.710
that's really, really hard

00:24:39.710 --> 00:24:43.430
to build into any education
platform, education system

00:24:43.430 --> 00:24:45.950
is that you're unlikely to remember

00:24:45.950 --> 00:24:48.100
most of the information
you're ever exposed to

00:24:48.100 --> 00:24:49.040
at any one time.

00:24:49.040 --> 00:24:50.570
And you've got to come back to it

00:24:50.570 --> 00:24:53.920
and anybody who's ever
crammed for a test knows

00:24:53.920 --> 00:24:56.070
that you're able, at a point in time,

00:24:56.070 --> 00:25:00.100
to perform as if you
have learned something,

00:25:00.100 --> 00:25:03.500
AKA perhaps from certain
dimensions, develop mastery of it.

00:25:03.500 --> 00:25:07.330
But if it hasn't actually
changed your long-term memory,

00:25:07.330 --> 00:25:10.250
i.e. what it is that you
store as your knowledge,

00:25:10.250 --> 00:25:12.170
then you haven't gained that mastery.

00:25:12.170 --> 00:25:13.960
And I'm sure Khan Academy doesn't do this,

00:25:13.960 --> 00:25:15.740
but there are a lot of other platforms

00:25:15.740 --> 00:25:19.400
where mastery means take a
quiz at the end of something.

00:25:19.400 --> 00:25:21.170
And if you score above a certain thing,

00:25:21.170 --> 00:25:23.580
then you've demonstrated mastery

00:25:23.580 --> 00:25:25.010
and on you are to the next subject.

00:25:25.010 --> 00:25:28.160
So I think that's an area where, sort of,

00:25:28.160 --> 00:25:31.320
I would love to see more
technology enthusiasts

00:25:31.320 --> 00:25:33.430
in education have a deeper understanding

00:25:33.430 --> 00:25:37.960
of what learning science says or suggests.

00:25:37.960 --> 00:25:40.620
- Well, I cannot let that just lie there

00:25:40.620 --> 00:25:43.960
because in Khan Academy, we in
fact have mastery challenges.

00:25:43.960 --> 00:25:46.400
So initially you take a quiz

00:25:46.400 --> 00:25:48.820
and get some level of proficiency,

00:25:48.820 --> 00:25:51.680
but then after a period of time,

00:25:51.680 --> 00:25:53.430
you'll have a mastery challenge comes up

00:25:53.430 --> 00:25:58.260
that's going to bring back
skills from previous things

00:25:58.260 --> 00:26:01.660
that you have achieved initial mastery at

00:26:01.660 --> 00:26:04.650
and give you some more
questions about those,

00:26:04.650 --> 00:26:08.213
doing that spaced practice,
that kind of spiral review.

00:26:09.220 --> 00:26:11.270
Absolutely, because of
all of that research,

00:26:11.270 --> 00:26:13.850
it's not just a one time, you're done,

00:26:13.850 --> 00:26:16.810
but there's a forgetting curve
and you need to think about

00:26:16.810 --> 00:26:21.610
how to help learners keep that
information fresh over time.

00:26:21.610 --> 00:26:25.213
So we absolutely are thinking
about that in what we do, too.

00:26:26.120 --> 00:26:26.953
- Great, I'm glad to hear it.

00:26:26.953 --> 00:26:29.440
- That said, I'm gonna pivot us back

00:26:29.440 --> 00:26:33.565
so you can tell a little
bit more about what you do.

00:26:33.565 --> 00:26:37.400
I appreciate it, thought,
I love this conversation.

00:26:37.400 --> 00:26:42.400
So in thinking about teacher
preparation programs,

00:26:43.040 --> 00:26:46.910
how do you go about working
with different schools

00:26:46.910 --> 00:26:48.973
and bringing them together?

00:26:50.830 --> 00:26:53.480
- Yeah, sorry if I hijacked the interview,

00:26:53.480 --> 00:26:56.730
but I'd like this to be bilateral

00:26:56.730 --> 00:26:59.320
rather than just one-sided.

00:26:59.320 --> 00:27:02.860
So we do a lot of different
things with schools of education

00:27:02.860 --> 00:27:04.320
and leaders in school of education,

00:27:04.320 --> 00:27:07.010
but per the conversation we're having now,

00:27:07.010 --> 00:27:11.210
the thing that we do is bring
together networks of programs

00:27:11.210 --> 00:27:13.900
that want to embed learning science

00:27:13.900 --> 00:27:17.820
into what we sometimes
call the arc of experiences

00:27:17.820 --> 00:27:19.170
that a teacher candidate,

00:27:19.170 --> 00:27:22.120
someone who's preparing to
become a teacher goes through

00:27:22.120 --> 00:27:24.490
from the very beginning
until the very end.

00:27:24.490 --> 00:27:27.620
And that work takes like multiple years,

00:27:27.620 --> 00:27:30.060
involves multiple faculty members

00:27:30.060 --> 00:27:32.200
and typically the Dean of Education,

00:27:32.200 --> 00:27:34.450
at least signing off on the project

00:27:34.450 --> 00:27:39.120
and then really working hard
to both figure out what,

00:27:39.120 --> 00:27:41.700
if anything, is being done presently

00:27:41.700 --> 00:27:45.220
to get those ideas into the
heads of future teachers

00:27:45.220 --> 00:27:47.980
and then figure out, well,
how can we do more of that

00:27:47.980 --> 00:27:49.300
and how will we figure out

00:27:49.300 --> 00:27:51.576
whether we've made improvement with that?

00:27:51.576 --> 00:27:54.290
We have something now that we've developed

00:27:54.290 --> 00:27:56.630
around sort of the six
principles that we have.

00:27:56.630 --> 00:27:59.310
And I'll talk about those in

00:27:59.310 --> 00:28:01.140
more particularity in a second here,

00:28:01.140 --> 00:28:04.890
but we have sort of these
modules as we call them

00:28:04.890 --> 00:28:08.840
where we sort of introduce a
concept from learning science,

00:28:08.840 --> 00:28:13.770
set up a series of ways
of engaging with it,

00:28:13.770 --> 00:28:17.680
not just read and regurgitate
back the principle,

00:28:17.680 --> 00:28:20.820
but think about it in
the context of a variety

00:28:20.820 --> 00:28:23.740
of different situations
that a teacher is likely

00:28:23.740 --> 00:28:27.770
to encounter and then move towards a place

00:28:27.770 --> 00:28:31.220
where both teacher educators,
the ones responsible

00:28:31.220 --> 00:28:33.170
for preparing people to be teachers

00:28:33.170 --> 00:28:36.990
and teacher candidates are
actually enacting and working

00:28:36.990 --> 00:28:41.380
with other students or other humans

00:28:41.380 --> 00:28:43.900
to actually apply the
principles in practice.

00:28:43.900 --> 00:28:46.110
And then from that, getting feedback

00:28:46.110 --> 00:28:48.720
and coming back and saying,
"Okay, did that work?

00:28:48.720 --> 00:28:50.320
Did it not work?

00:28:50.320 --> 00:28:53.030
Did you understand what
you were trying to do

00:28:53.030 --> 00:28:56.050
and why it may have worked
or why it did not succeed?"

00:28:56.050 --> 00:28:58.750
And now hopefully get feedback on that

00:28:58.750 --> 00:28:59.940
so that you can go try it again

00:28:59.940 --> 00:29:02.560
and hopefully practice and get better.

00:29:02.560 --> 00:29:06.380
So that's the nature of the
work as mentioned earlier,

00:29:06.380 --> 00:29:08.480
we're both assessing at the beginning,

00:29:08.480 --> 00:29:11.350
throughout the whole
process and at the end

00:29:11.350 --> 00:29:12.790
to see whether there's improvements.

00:29:12.790 --> 00:29:14.610
And we have enough data now

00:29:14.610 --> 00:29:16.790
since we started launching these networks

00:29:16.790 --> 00:29:19.610
to see pretty dramatic improvements

00:29:19.610 --> 00:29:22.731
in the areas where programs have focused.

00:29:22.731 --> 00:29:25.180
And so, I'll pause there

00:29:25.180 --> 00:29:26.880
if you have any questions about that,

00:29:26.880 --> 00:29:29.780
but I can sort of talk
about a particular principle

00:29:29.780 --> 00:29:32.170
that's become pretty focal
for a lot of programs

00:29:32.170 --> 00:29:34.970
and sort of what that looks
like in the work that we do.

00:29:35.970 --> 00:29:37.350
- Yeah, one of my questions,

00:29:37.350 --> 00:29:39.800
to go back to this point
about mastering skills,

00:29:39.800 --> 00:29:41.220
we're actually asking teachers

00:29:41.220 --> 00:29:43.870
to master these skills
related to teaching,

00:29:43.870 --> 00:29:45.720
based on learning science.

00:29:45.720 --> 00:29:49.880
How do you think about
designing programs so that

00:29:49.880 --> 00:29:54.250
it's not just, "Here's this
skill, here's the thing.

00:29:54.250 --> 00:29:56.160
Okay, now we're moving
on to the next thing."

00:29:56.160 --> 00:29:58.530
And keep it kind of woven through.

00:29:58.530 --> 00:29:59.679
- Yeah.

00:29:59.679 --> 00:30:01.510
I mean, this is the hard part,

00:30:01.510 --> 00:30:06.170
and it's particularly hard
working with schools of education

00:30:06.170 --> 00:30:09.720
that are inside institutions
of higher education,

00:30:09.720 --> 00:30:12.200
universities, as we typically call them.

00:30:12.200 --> 00:30:13.860
And the reason for that is,

00:30:13.860 --> 00:30:15.850
is that if you think back to anyone

00:30:15.850 --> 00:30:19.270
who's experienced college and university,

00:30:19.270 --> 00:30:21.920
you had a bunch of courses, right?

00:30:21.920 --> 00:30:24.770
And maybe they all added up to a major,

00:30:24.770 --> 00:30:27.890
but each of the courses was sort of

00:30:29.420 --> 00:30:32.790
project and owned by
whoever the professor was.

00:30:32.790 --> 00:30:36.330
Or maybe the professor farmed
it out to some grad students

00:30:36.330 --> 00:30:38.410
who then taught the class.

00:30:38.410 --> 00:30:41.760
And so what you have
typically had is a series

00:30:41.760 --> 00:30:45.700
of discrete things that don't braid

00:30:45.700 --> 00:30:48.520
and do not interleave,
to use a term that comes

00:30:48.520 --> 00:30:51.620
from learning science
that basically means just,

00:30:51.620 --> 00:30:53.950
keep coming back and
weaving through ideas.

00:30:53.950 --> 00:30:57.160
And don't just focus on
one thing and move on.

00:30:57.160 --> 00:31:00.600
So what we try to do is bring together

00:31:00.600 --> 00:31:02.040
all the people involved

00:31:02.040 --> 00:31:05.800
in the whole arc of experiences,
again, as we call it.

00:31:05.800 --> 00:31:10.390
And that means getting
together, tenured faculty,

00:31:10.390 --> 00:31:14.360
non-tenured faculty, what
are called the adjuncts,

00:31:14.360 --> 00:31:18.010
who are often sort of
hired on to do a lot of

00:31:18.010 --> 00:31:19.680
sort of the practicum training.

00:31:19.680 --> 00:31:22.470
It means bringing in,
and this is really hard,

00:31:22.470 --> 00:31:25.500
bringing in the teachers
who are in K-12 schools,

00:31:25.500 --> 00:31:27.810
who are involved in
mentoring the teachers.

00:31:27.810 --> 00:31:29.470
So you've got to get everybody

00:31:29.470 --> 00:31:33.220
at the table agreeing on,

00:31:33.220 --> 00:31:35.770
agreeing on the importance
of embedding these principles

00:31:35.770 --> 00:31:39.460
in all of the aspects of
preparing someone to be a teacher.

00:31:39.460 --> 00:31:41.200
So it's hard work,

00:31:41.200 --> 00:31:44.040
but it's also really amazing
work when it pays off,

00:31:44.040 --> 00:31:48.020
because you can actually talk
to these teacher candidates

00:31:48.020 --> 00:31:49.967
at the end of it, people who are often

00:31:49.967 --> 00:31:52.140
21, 22 years old,

00:31:52.140 --> 00:31:54.230
college seniors who are about to graduate

00:31:54.230 --> 00:31:56.560
and the level of sophistication they have

00:31:56.560 --> 00:31:58.670
in talking about their practice

00:31:58.670 --> 00:32:00.790
and the confidence that they have

00:32:00.790 --> 00:32:03.490
in sort of what sort of
teacher they want to be,

00:32:03.490 --> 00:32:06.440
what sort of activities they
want to give to their students,

00:32:06.440 --> 00:32:10.239
what sort of learning they
want to see take place,

00:32:10.239 --> 00:32:12.380
their beliefs about the capabilities

00:32:12.380 --> 00:32:14.484
of all of their students.

00:32:14.484 --> 00:32:15.680
It's inspiring.

00:32:15.680 --> 00:32:18.140
I mean, I know I'm biased,

00:32:18.140 --> 00:32:21.120
but it's what gets up everybody

00:32:21.120 --> 00:32:22.510
who does this work at Deans for Impact.

00:32:22.510 --> 00:32:25.640
I think it's probably the
single biggest motivator for us.

00:32:25.640 --> 00:32:28.340
So that's what it looks like
and that's what it takes.

00:32:30.040 --> 00:32:32.170
- That sounds great.

00:32:32.170 --> 00:32:35.258
We've already gone through our 30 minutes.

00:32:35.258 --> 00:32:36.091
- What?

00:32:36.091 --> 00:32:39.437
I didn't even get to talk about
specific principles, my bad.

00:32:39.437 --> 00:32:41.458
I asked questions, I
shouldn't have done it.

00:32:41.458 --> 00:32:43.091
- Do you have a couple of minutes?

00:32:43.091 --> 00:32:43.924
- Yeah.

00:32:43.924 --> 00:32:44.757
- We can do a specific principle?

00:32:44.757 --> 00:32:47.940
Let's do that, go ahead
and let's talk about that.

00:32:47.940 --> 00:32:49.910
- Well, I just wanted to
talk about one principle

00:32:49.910 --> 00:32:52.090
in particular that I'd mentioned earlier,

00:32:52.090 --> 00:32:53.910
that's proven to be
particularly important.

00:32:53.910 --> 00:32:56.820
And I think there's a reason for that.

00:32:56.820 --> 00:32:59.420
One of the six principles
from learning science

00:32:59.420 --> 00:33:01.080
that we've articulated

00:33:01.080 --> 00:33:03.980
is something we call deepening
meaning and learning,

00:33:03.980 --> 00:33:05.980
and that's kind of vague and hazy,

00:33:05.980 --> 00:33:08.790
but what we mean by
that is kind of simple.

00:33:08.790 --> 00:33:09.978
And that is,

00:33:09.978 --> 00:33:14.400
what is it that you want
your students to think about

00:33:14.400 --> 00:33:18.330
and how will you design the experience

00:33:18.330 --> 00:33:22.110
they have in your classroom,
so they think about that thing?

00:33:22.110 --> 00:33:24.320
And let me give you a
very tangible example

00:33:24.320 --> 00:33:26.050
and it kind of connects
to what I was just saying

00:33:26.050 --> 00:33:30.020
about the inspiration
that comes from this work.

00:33:30.020 --> 00:33:32.970
I just was on the phone
about a couple of weeks ago

00:33:32.970 --> 00:33:36.080
with a soon to be teacher
in North Carolina,

00:33:36.080 --> 00:33:39.520
who was doing part of her
teacher training in a school

00:33:39.520 --> 00:33:43.983
in Charlotte and a veteran
12 year teacher asked her

00:33:44.900 --> 00:33:46.080
to do a particular task

00:33:46.080 --> 00:33:48.560
with her eighth grade science students.

00:33:48.560 --> 00:33:53.560
And the task was get some
plaster of paris, roll it around,

00:33:54.430 --> 00:33:58.360
take a plastic dinosaur, stick
it in the plaster of paris,

00:33:58.360 --> 00:34:01.750
wait for it to dry and
now you have a fossil.

00:34:01.750 --> 00:34:06.750
Now, that might be fun and
interesting to an eighth grader.

00:34:07.130 --> 00:34:09.060
I have my doubts,

00:34:09.060 --> 00:34:12.820
but what it won't do is teach
them anything meaningful

00:34:12.820 --> 00:34:15.270
about how fossils get made.

00:34:15.270 --> 00:34:18.010
And what was really remarkable is that

00:34:18.010 --> 00:34:22.800
this soon to be teacher
could see all of this

00:34:22.800 --> 00:34:25.780
and could say this isn't
the sort of thinking

00:34:25.780 --> 00:34:27.370
that I want my students to do.

00:34:27.370 --> 00:34:29.530
There are five different types of fossils.

00:34:29.530 --> 00:34:31.460
I want them to understand
what the differences

00:34:31.460 --> 00:34:33.650
between those five different
types of fossils are.

00:34:33.650 --> 00:34:35.210
I want them to understand the differences

00:34:35.210 --> 00:34:37.240
in what it takes to make those fossils.

00:34:37.240 --> 00:34:40.470
I want them to understand what
different things we can learn

00:34:40.470 --> 00:34:43.040
and know about our society today

00:34:43.040 --> 00:34:45.240
because of those different
types of fossils.

00:34:46.190 --> 00:34:48.240
This is someone, I don't
know exactly how old she is,

00:34:48.240 --> 00:34:49.460
but she's a college senior.

00:34:49.460 --> 00:34:52.360
And she's looking at
something that, unfortunately,

00:34:52.360 --> 00:34:56.040
some 12 year veteran teacher
is thinking is a good activity

00:34:56.040 --> 00:34:57.917
for eighth grade science
students and saying,

00:34:57.917 --> 00:35:01.980
"Ah, not for me and not for
the students I'm gonna teach."

00:35:01.980 --> 00:35:06.640
So that's the principle
that more than any other,

00:35:06.640 --> 00:35:09.080
we have seen the programs
we're working with,

00:35:09.080 --> 00:35:11.330
teacher candidates struggle, and frankly,

00:35:11.330 --> 00:35:13.570
even sometimes teacher educators struggle.

00:35:13.570 --> 00:35:16.500
And I think more than
just about anything else

00:35:16.500 --> 00:35:17.820
in education broadly,

00:35:17.820 --> 00:35:20.450
if we could get everybody involved

00:35:20.450 --> 00:35:22.480
to really focus on the question,

00:35:22.480 --> 00:35:25.750
what is it that is important
for students to think about

00:35:25.750 --> 00:35:28.210
and how I get them to think about that

00:35:28.210 --> 00:35:32.203
in ways that they will truly
learn it, to our earlier point.

00:35:33.490 --> 00:35:35.640
I think nothing else could
be more transformative

00:35:35.640 --> 00:35:38.760
to our education system and
to frankly, our citizenry.

00:35:38.760 --> 00:35:39.593
I really do.

00:35:41.780 --> 00:35:42.819
- I love that.

00:35:42.819 --> 00:35:44.350
And I love that as a prompt for thinking

00:35:44.350 --> 00:35:47.370
about technology in the classroom too,

00:35:47.370 --> 00:35:51.503
because there's real limits
to what technology can do.

00:35:53.518 --> 00:35:56.020
And Khan Academy really
ready to acknowledge that.

00:35:56.020 --> 00:35:59.110
And it's really hard from our side

00:35:59.110 --> 00:36:01.160
to know what students in the classroom are

00:36:01.160 --> 00:36:02.030
actually thinking about.

00:36:02.030 --> 00:36:05.210
So we can provide those
prompts and those activities,

00:36:05.210 --> 00:36:07.670
but it takes the teacher in the classroom,

00:36:07.670 --> 00:36:10.420
working with those
students, asking them why,

00:36:10.420 --> 00:36:15.060
asking them what made you say that?

00:36:15.060 --> 00:36:18.230
All of that to really understand
what students are thinking,

00:36:18.230 --> 00:36:20.740
and if they're getting that meaning from

00:36:20.740 --> 00:36:25.120
what the prompt was and
what those places were.

00:36:25.120 --> 00:36:27.110
So really interesting.

00:36:27.110 --> 00:36:28.300
- Yeah, you're totally right about that.

00:36:28.300 --> 00:36:30.810
And I thank you for raising
it and at the same time,

00:36:30.810 --> 00:36:32.304
I actually think there's a number

00:36:32.304 --> 00:36:34.590
of ways in which technology,

00:36:34.590 --> 00:36:37.390
when you have an understanding
of learning science

00:36:37.390 --> 00:36:38.680
becomes really empowering,

00:36:38.680 --> 00:36:42.190
because now you're not just
being told to use technology

00:36:42.190 --> 00:36:44.590
by a superintendent who was sold something

00:36:44.590 --> 00:36:45.980
by a vendor somewhere.

00:36:45.980 --> 00:36:49.300
You're thinking about how will
this be something that leads

00:36:49.300 --> 00:36:51.530
to the sort of learning I
want to have with my students.

00:36:51.530 --> 00:36:54.230
And I didn't think I was
gonna tell the story,

00:36:54.230 --> 00:36:56.030
because I haven't thought
about it for a while,

00:36:56.030 --> 00:36:58.600
but I think it's befitting
our conversation.

00:36:58.600 --> 00:37:01.640
One of the interesting
tools I've seen used

00:37:01.640 --> 00:37:03.050
a little less so nowadays,

00:37:03.050 --> 00:37:05.470
but there's tools that allow people

00:37:05.470 --> 00:37:07.490
to become their own Sal Khan.

00:37:07.490 --> 00:37:11.120
You can do sort of your
own little casting.

00:37:11.120 --> 00:37:14.210
Well, there are some
brilliant teacher educators

00:37:14.210 --> 00:37:16.210
and professional development specialists

00:37:16.210 --> 00:37:19.120
who have taken to giving kids a tablet

00:37:19.120 --> 00:37:22.630
and asking them to be their own Sal Khan,

00:37:22.630 --> 00:37:24.270
as they're working through a problem,

00:37:24.270 --> 00:37:25.940
and then they record it.

00:37:25.940 --> 00:37:29.690
And then you have an
artifact that you can look at

00:37:29.690 --> 00:37:31.623
that literally gives you sort of,

00:37:32.490 --> 00:37:34.540
you can hear the student talking through,

00:37:34.540 --> 00:37:36.080
you can see them doing the math equation.

00:37:36.080 --> 00:37:37.840
You can see them erasing things,

00:37:37.840 --> 00:37:39.997
and you can go back and
look at it and start to say,

00:37:39.997 --> 00:37:42.200
"Okay, where was the misconception?

00:37:42.200 --> 00:37:43.080
Where did that come in?

00:37:43.080 --> 00:37:45.460
What were they thinking
when they did this?

00:37:45.460 --> 00:37:47.170
Why do you think they did this?

00:37:47.170 --> 00:37:49.020
What would you do as the teacher,

00:37:49.020 --> 00:37:51.300
if you thought they were
having that misconception?

00:37:51.300 --> 00:37:53.270
What would you do to
check if you were mistaken

00:37:53.270 --> 00:37:54.420
about that misconception?"

00:37:54.420 --> 00:37:56.220
So it's a way of saying,

00:37:56.220 --> 00:37:59.060
if we get every kid to be like Sal Khan

00:37:59.060 --> 00:38:02.737
with a little tablet, we might
be making some real progress.

00:38:02.737 --> 00:38:04.416
(Kristen laughs)

00:38:04.416 --> 00:38:06.380
- That sounds great, love it.

00:38:06.380 --> 00:38:08.903
All right, I will ask to close.

00:38:10.050 --> 00:38:13.907
For you to give us one note of optimism,

00:38:13.907 --> 00:38:16.410
something that you see that's promising

00:38:16.410 --> 00:38:20.620
or gives you hope in our current times.

00:38:20.620 --> 00:38:23.890
- Yeah, well, I may have
already stepped on it

00:38:23.890 --> 00:38:26.810
by mentioning that teacher,
soon to be teacher,

00:38:26.810 --> 00:38:29.337
I was speaking to earlier,
but she's not alone.

00:38:29.337 --> 00:38:32.630
There are so many teachers right now

00:38:32.630 --> 00:38:35.680
who are starting to get
more and more interested

00:38:35.680 --> 00:38:37.560
in these ideas from learning science

00:38:37.560 --> 00:38:39.440
and it's global, it's global.

00:38:39.440 --> 00:38:41.660
It's really a conversation
that's happening.

00:38:41.660 --> 00:38:45.130
And we get inquiries from
teachers and teacher educators,

00:38:45.130 --> 00:38:47.970
and policy makers from all over the globe.

00:38:47.970 --> 00:38:49.790
And without exception,

00:38:49.790 --> 00:38:53.010
their inquiries are always
about the science of learning

00:38:53.010 --> 00:38:55.600
and what it means for their teaching

00:38:55.600 --> 00:38:57.340
or for their education system.

00:38:57.340 --> 00:39:00.380
And so there's a movement happening

00:39:00.380 --> 00:39:02.080
and we're lucky to be part of it.

00:39:02.080 --> 00:39:06.030
And I'm grateful for the chance
to have this conversation,

00:39:06.030 --> 00:39:09.270
because I hope everybody
who's listening and watching

00:39:09.270 --> 00:39:11.490
will take a peak and
start to get interested

00:39:11.490 --> 00:39:13.390
in these ideas, if you aren't already.

00:39:14.750 --> 00:39:16.010
- Great, thank you so much.

00:39:16.010 --> 00:39:17.570
Thank you for the work you do.

00:39:17.570 --> 00:39:20.140
And also for joining us
to talk about it today.

00:39:20.140 --> 00:39:21.920
Loved the conversation.

00:39:21.920 --> 00:39:25.250
Thank you and best wishes in the new year.

00:39:25.250 --> 00:39:26.083
- Thanks so much, Kristen.

00:39:26.083 --> 00:39:28.130
I really enjoyed it, thank you, again.

